<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">St. Stephen's Cathedral, Passau</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">St. Stephen's Cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passau">Passau</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, is the baroque cathedral of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Passau, rebuilt after a fire of 1662 by Italian architects led by Carlo Lurago. Its organ, with nearly 18,000 pipes, is one of the largest church organs in the world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/st-stephens-cathedral-passau.jpg" alt="St. Stephen's Cathedral, Passau" width="320" height="232" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aconcagua · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Passauer_Dom.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Churches have stood on the site since 730. The fire of 1662 destroyed the previous building except for its late Gothic eastern part, which survives in the present church. The new cathedral, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> building about 100 metres long dedicated to Saint Stephen, was completed in 1693. Carlo Lurago drew up its overall plan, Giovanni Battista Carlone designed the interior decoration and Carpoforo Tencalla painted the frescoes. It is the seat of the Catholic Bishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passau">Passau</a> and the main church of his diocese.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Eight large bells hang in the north and south towers. The largest, the Pummerin of 7,550 kilograms, was cast in 1952 and hangs in the south tower with the Sturmerin of 5,300 kilograms, cast in 1733. The north tower holds the other six, among them the second bourdon, Misericordia, of 6,000 kilograms, and a ninth bell hangs near the sacristy door. Three of the bells strike the clock: the Elfuhrglocken chimes every quarter hour, and the Predigtglocke and the Sturmerin in turn strike the hours.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The cathedral's organ was once the largest in the world. Later instruments, among them the Wanamaker organ in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, have surpassed it, but it remains the largest church organ outside the United States. It is in fact several organs of different tonal styles, added to over the years, with 17,774 pipes and 233 registers that can all be played from the five-manual general console in the gallery; parts of it have their own mechanical-action or electric-action consoles, six consoles in all.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “St. Stephen's Cathedral, Passau” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After a fire in 1662 Italian architects rebuilt the city and St. <b>Stephen's Cathedral</b> in baroque style.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-passau">Prince-Bishopric of Passau</a></li></ul></section>
